<article class="env-lemma" id="01ZN">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01ZN">Lemma <span data-tag="01ZN">32.8.2</span>.</a> Notation and assumptions as in Situation <a href="/tag/081D" data-tag="081D">32.8.1</a>. If $f$ is affine, then there exists an index $i \geq 0$ such that $f_ i$ is affine. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $Y_0 = \bigcup _{j = 1, \ldots , m} V_{j, 0}$ be a finite affine open covering. Set $U_{j, 0} = f_0^{-1}(V_{j, 0})$. For $i \geq 0$ we denote $V_{j, i}$ the inverse image of $V_{j, 0}$ in $Y_ i$ and $U_{j, i} = f_ i^{-1}(V_{j, i})$. Similarly we have $U_ j = f^{-1}(V_ j)$. Then $U_ j = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _{i \geq 0} U_{j, i}$ (see Lemma <a href="/tag/01YX" data-tag="01YX">32.2.2</a>). Since $U_ j$ is affine by assumption we see that each $U_{j, i}$ is affine for $i$ large enough, see Lemma <a href="/tag/01Z6" data-tag="01Z6">32.4.13</a>. As there are finitely many $j$ we can pick an $i$ which works for all $j$. Thus $f_ i$ is affine for $i$ large enough, see Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/01S8" data-tag="01S8">29.11.3</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>